LESSON 6: MULTI-INPUT FORM SETUP - HANDS-ON
================================================================================
PART 1: OVERVIEW OF THE LESSON
================================================================================

WHAT YOU'LL LEARN IN THIS LESSON
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This lesson focuses on building and managing React forms containing multiple
input fields.

You will understand:

• Setting up multiple form inputs

• Managing related fields in one state object

• Creating a reusable onChange handler

• Using computed property names

• Handling different input types

• Handling text, email, phone, number, select, textarea, radio, and checkbox

• Submitting multiple form fields together

• Resetting multiple fields

• Validating multiple inputs

• Managing field-specific errors

• Displaying current form data

• Organizing larger forms into sections

• Creating scalable form patterns


WHY THIS LESSON IS IMPORTANT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Most real applications use more than one form field.

Examples:

• Registration Forms

• Login/Profile Forms

• Contact Forms

• Checkout Forms

• Search/Filter Forms

• Employee Forms

• Banking Forms

• Application Forms


A good multi-input pattern reduces repetitive code.

Instead of:

Field 1 → Handler 1
Field 2 → Handler 2
Field 3 → Handler 3


we can often use:

Multiple Fields
      ↓
One State Object
      ↓
One Generic Handler


================================================================================
PART 2: UNDERSTANDING MULTI-INPUT FORMS
================================================================================

WHAT IS A MULTI-INPUT FORM?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

A multi-input form contains multiple related controls.

Example:

First Name

Last Name

Email

Phone

Country

Message

Subscribe


A common React structure is:

formData
   │
   ├── firstName
   ├── lastName
   ├── email
   ├── phone
   ├── country
   ├── message
   └── subscribe


================================================================================
CORE PATTERN
================================================================================

STATE:

const [formData, setFormData] =
  useState({

    firstName: '',

    lastName: '',

    email: '',

    phone: ''

  })


INPUT:

<input
  name="firstName"
  value={formData.firstName}
  onChange={handleChange}
/>


GENERIC HANDLER:

const handleChange = (event) => {

  const {
    name,
    value
  } = event.target


  setFormData(previousData => ({

    ...previousData,

    [name]: value

  }))
}


================================================================================
WHY THE name ATTRIBUTE MATTERS
================================================================================

Input:

name="firstName"


State:

formData.firstName


Input:

name="email"


State:

formData.email


Therefore:

[name]: value


dynamically updates the matching property.


Example:

name:

"email"


value:

"john@example.com"


Then:

[name]: value


becomes conceptually:

email:
'john@example.com'


================================================================================
MULTI-INPUT VS SEPARATE STATE
================================================================================

SEPARATE STATE CAN BE VALID:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const [firstName, setFirstName] =
  useState('')


const [lastName, setLastName] =
  useState('')


const [email, setEmail] =
  useState('')


OBJECT STATE CAN ALSO BE VALID:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const [formData, setFormData] =
  useState({

    firstName: '',

    lastName: '',

    email: ''

  })


================================================================================
WHEN OBJECT STATE IS CONVENIENT
================================================================================

An object is useful when:

• The fields belong to the same form

• Many controls use similar update logic

• The entire object is validated/submitted together

• You want one generic handler

• Resetting everything together is convenient


IMPORTANT:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

There is no rule that every multi-input React form MUST use one state object.

Choose the state structure that best matches the form's behavior.


================================================================================
MULTI-INPUT FORM CHARACTERISTICS
================================================================================

+------------------------+---------------------------------------------+
| CONCEPT                | PURPOSE                                     |
+------------------------+---------------------------------------------+
| formData               | Related form values                         |
| name                   | Identifies state property                   |
| value                  | Controlled text-like value                  |
| checked                | Controlled checkbox/radio state             |
| handleChange           | Shared change handler                       |
| [name]                 | Computed property name                      |
| Spread                 | Preserves other form fields                 |
| Validation             | Checks submitted/current values             |
| errors                 | Field-specific error messages               |
| Reset                  | Restores initial form state                 |
+------------------------+---------------------------------------------+


